Well it's not quite that simple in the game. The example you use in the game
of lycanthropy. Lets say you get turned into a warewolf. Now there are two
ways you can fix it that I know of you can pray and it might get fixed or
you can eat wolfsbane. Now the first is not certain. I had a good tourist
who failed to do this twice. As for wolfsbane it's no where near common. Now
consider the fact that you may not want to. A warewolf character can summon
wolves some of which can be pretty tough. It's feasable you could get this
at a low enough level for wolves to be useful as they are which probably
isn't as low as you'd think. Even the lesser werecreatures can summon
monsters that can be used as a shield. If you have a method of polymorphing
a pet then the ability is multiplied. Having a purple worm for a pet as a
level 2 wizard is incredible. So while curing it is a simple if/then weather
or you want to cure it is not.

There are many such situations like this in nethack. So they tend to add up
and the sum is greater then the whole of it's parts.
The game enjoys a great simplicity in how it works and great complexity in
game play. Note I am talking about the play end of things not the code.

Now what is it you want from the game. You want more open ended item
interaction. I'm not sure what that means. Everything in the game has to be
programmed in. Many situations have many different effects. From what
intrinsic you get when you eat a corpse that grants multiple intrinsics to
prayer. You could  have more equipment creation but once again everything
you create has to be coded in. Also from what I've heard this tends to be
unbalancing when tried. Either it is far to powerful and it's not worth
doing anything else, or it's so limited as to be useless. I think nethack
strikes a good balance with its combination of dipping holy water/alchamy/
polypiling/wish, but many people think that this is to powerful. Or at least
aspects of it.

It sounds like you are a tabletop rpger. If not you should find a game and
try it out. The fact that a tabletop rpg has a human in control gives it far
more options in the what do you want to do catagory. With any game your
limited to what you put in the code.



